WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Good morning, John!

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:03.949
There's something special about humans, and
this is subjectively true.

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:07.790
Some people think that their sports team is
particularly special because they know the

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:12.000
whole history of that team and all of the
intricate details of the players on it.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.440
But they don't know all those details for
other teams, so they see their team as the

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.530
best, most interesting, most unique thing.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.650
That's cognitive bias and that's fine.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:20.650
Sports, whoooo!

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.820
All sports teams are equally special, meaning
none of them are.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:25.720
But that is not true of humans, as a species.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.810
Among the immense variety of life on Earth,
there are lots of organisms that are good

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:29.860
at particular things.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.180
Like giraffes are super tall, and cheetahs
and antelope are super fast.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.190
And some methanogens eukaryotes are particularly
good at surviving and thriving in boiling

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:38.190
water.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.750
But humans aren't like particularly good at
certain things, one or two.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.230
We're good at all the things.

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:46.620
We can dive into the crushing depths of the
bottom of the ocean and travel faster than

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.620
any other animal.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:51.060
We can even escape the safe little bubble
of our atmosphere and not die.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.149
This uniqueness about humans is obvious.

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:55.130
Even if it weren't true, I think we would
think it.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:59.100
That has led a lot of people to think different
things about what the thing is that makes

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.170
us so exceptional.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:04.989
There's like, opposable thumbs that people
have thought maybe, which is ridiculous cuz

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:05.989
raccoons.

00:01:05.989 --> 00:01:09.290
Then for a while we thought that the thing
that set us apart was tool use, which no because

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.350
like ravens and primates and dolphins.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:16.150
If it's not that, then maybe it's self-awareness;
maybe it's knowing that I am a thing who is

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:20.780
me and that allows me to think differently
about the world, but also, it seems like a

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.890
lot of animals are also self-aware: that's
not it.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:26.430
Well, I've decided, and I don't know what
gives me the right to do this, but I have

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:30.780
decided that the thing that sets us apart
as people, that makes us capable of air conditioning

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:35.740
and hot showers and lunar landings and nuclear
war can be summed up in one word, and that

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:37.330
word is 'stories'.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:41.049
If this sounds a little froofy to you, let
me remind you that there was a time before

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:44.880
email, before phones, before newspapers, before
the written word even.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.010
When humanity was first benefiting from the
massive utility of passing tremendous amounts

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:55.340
of information from generation to generation,
the vehicle for the passing of that information

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.710
was the story.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:00.670
Stories were, and I think that they still
are, how we define ourselves and our culture

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:02.790
and even our technology and science.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:07.170
Every human society that wants to behave differently
first has to change the stories that they

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.170
tell.

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:13.560
Stories, in songs, in books, on the stage,
on podcasts, around the campfire help us define

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:14.650
who we are.

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.170
We are made of stories.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:19.980
So when I realized we had a full time staff
working on VidCon, but they had certain lull

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.270
periods in their year, because VidCon only
happens once a year.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:26.490
And those people were excited about creating
another conference in a different place and

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.910
a different time of the year, celebrating
a different thing.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.190
I had to think: "What is a cultural institution
that is as important to me, or more important

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:35.180
to me than the institution of online video?

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.060
Which is also very important to me."

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.650
So I went with 'stories', which is, admittedly,
broad.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:44.349
So this year, 2015, on October 9th and 10th
in Minneapolis, Minnesota at the Minneapolis

00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:50.310
Convention Center we will be celebrating stories
at a new event called NerdCon: Stories.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:54.470
And yes, we are leaving the door open for
maybe future NerdCon: Other Things.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:58.019
We'll have panels, and talks, and performances,
and question and answer sessions, and a tiny

00:02:58.019 --> 00:03:01.780
little vendor room and yes: this is going
to be much smaller of an affair than VidCon

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:02.780
is.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.530
But, our special guests, at least in my book,
are certainly not a step down.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:06.610
Hank and John Green will be there, so, there's
that.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:10.420
We'll have fantasy authors like Pat Rothfuss,
Cassandra Claire, and Holly Black and Mary

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:14.670
Robinette Kowal, Sci-fi authors like John
Scalzi and Paolo Bacigalupi, Young Adult authors,

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.290
like Jacqueline Woodson and Bethany Perkins.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:20.290
And also lots of things that aren't books,
like musicians like Kimya Dawson and Dessa

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.730
Darling, shows like Welcome to Night Vale,
and Wits, and Story Collider.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.520
Organizations that promote stories, and game
developers, and poets, and playwrights!

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.610
And this is just the beginning of our list,
we have a lot of time to continue planning

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.480
and adding new people to our event.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.019
We're very excited about Nerdcon: Stories,
I think it's going to be... an exceptional

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:36.019
event.

00:03:36.019 --> 00:03:38.849
I'm basically, a little bit designing it just
so I will enjoy it, let's be honest.

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:42.110
To people who are able to come: We are expecting
this event to sell out pretty quickly, cuz

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:44.910
it's not very big, so Nerdcon.com to get tickets.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:49.170
For people who can't: Stories are universal,
and they are everywhere, and I encourage you

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:53.430
to participate in this culture of stories,
and just to revel in the general wonderfulness

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.810
and humanity of the institution of stories.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.770
John, I will see you on Tuesday.

